The first assertion is obtained by ``filling in'' the hole $i$, then the section under consideration becomes the section of $U\cup\{i\}=U'$ associated to the point $i$, and it is therefore distinct from the one associated to the \deleted{points} other points of $U'$, that is, to the points of $U$ --- a fortiori before passing to the quotient by the normal subgroup generated by $L_i$. One \unsure{will want} in the $\unsure{=|\overline m}$ way to convince oneself that the conjugacy classes of the splittings associated to an $L_{i_1}$ and an $L_{i_2}$, $i_1\ne i_2$, are distinct --- this works in all cases except the case of type $(0,3)$, --- for by filling in two holes, one ends up with type $(0,1)$, where one does not have the injectivity result. But one can get around it another way, provided one admits that for a splitting of (2), making $\Gamma$ act on $\pi$, one has
\[
(3)\qquad\pi^{\Gamma^\circ}=L_i
\]
(hence $\pi^\Gamma=(1)$, incidentally) --- a result which seems highly plausible to me. This
